            price2_policy: `Rate sheet, documented exceptions and a monthly review by group`,
            r1_brief: `Craig Ostrander’s 2027 retail plan doubles the account-opening goals and asks every branch for a Spanish-language campaign for the free checking account. The six personal bankers’ incentive plan: keep the bank’s product-count goals with monthly payouts per account opened (volume +2%; half a finding; $30,000 more exposure for unauthorized accounts); a scorecard that pays on balances still active after 90 days, customer surveys and a compliance quality check, with clawback of incentives for accounts closed unused or opened without consent ($60,000 for the reviews and the redesign; volume +1%; households +3); salary only, no incentives (volume −2%); or the regional plan — double the goals, a daily leaderboard and performance plans for bankers below target (volume +4%; $20,000 of contests; 1 finding; households −3; $110,000 more exposure). Language: service and documents in English with a telephone interpreter on request; a written language-access policy with two certified bilingual bankers, a bilingual teller on every shift, the account agreement and key disclosures in Spanish and complaints taken in Spanish ($70,000; volume +3%; CRA index +6; households +4); the Spanish radio and billboard campaign with English-only staff and documents ($25,000; volume +2%; 1 finding; households −2); or steer customers to the app and close the teller line on Saturdays (saves $90,000; volume −1%; CRA index −6; households −3).`,
            r1_note: `Opening accounts or cards without the customer’s consent is an unfair and abusive practice (12 U.S.C. §§ 5531, 5536). Advertising in Spanish a service delivered only in English can mislead customers.`,
            r1_title: `Round 1 · Monday 4 January 2027: the sales plan and the language of service`,
            r2_brief: `On Tuesday 16 February the review of the branch’s 2026 HMDA data showed that the loan officers’ discretionary pricing exceptions left Hispanic borrowers on average 0.30 percentage points worse off than non-Hispanic white borrowers with similar credit, and that the branch’s own tracts had fewer mortgage applications than any other branch’s. Pricing: leave the discretion as it is (1 finding; CRA index −2); a written rate sheet, exceptions only for documented reasons (a competing offer in writing, relationship balances), a second review of every exception and a monthly comparison by HMDA group ($20,000; CRA index +2; households +1); centralised pricing with no exceptions at all ($10,000; volume −2%); or a 680 minimum credit score and no mortgage or small-business loan under $100,000 (saves $15,000; volume −1%; half a finding; CRA index −10; households −2). The CRA plan: none; a first-time homebuyer programme with down-payment grants through the Federal Home Loan Bank, homebuyer education in Spanish with Casa Adelante, a HUD-approved counselling agency, and SBA-guaranteed microloans to Maryvale businesses ($90,000 including higher provisions; volume +2%; CRA index +14; households +3); buy $4 million of CRA-qualifying mortgages originated by another lender ($30,000 of yield given up; CRA index +6); or move the loan officer and the mailers to the higher-income ZIP codes outside Maryvale (saves $20,000; volume +1%; 2 findings; about $50,000 as the branch’s share of a redlining settlement; CRA index −12; households −3).`,
            r2_note: `The Equal Credit Opportunity Act forbids discrimination on national origin in any credit transaction (15 U.S.C. § 1691). The CRA measures how a bank meets the credit needs of its whole community, including low- and moderate-income neighbourhoods (12 U.S.C. §§ 2901–2908).`,
            r2_title: `Round 2 · Monday 5 April 2027: loan pricing and the CRA lending plan`,
            r3_brief: `On Thursday 20 May, Arturo Celaya, owner of Carnicería El Saguaro, deposited $9,500 in cash for the fourth time in two weeks, after asking a teller what happens above $10,000. In June, Efraín Ledesma, 67, a customer for thirty years, sold his tire shop for $780,000 and asks you whether “all of it” will be FDIC insured at the branch. Arturo’s cash: nothing — he is a good customer and no deposit reaches $10,000 (about $20,000 of exam remediation; 1 finding); tellers aggregate same-day cash for the currency transaction reports, the pattern goes to Denise Okafor-Hale, the BSA officer, who decides on a suspicious activity report, nobody tells the customer and the branch keeps serving the business while updating its cash profile ($5,000); close his account and tell him it is because of the reports (volume −0.5%; 1.5 findings; households −2); or a teller tells him to keep each deposit under $10,000 “so there is no paperwork” (about $40,000; 3 findings). The Ledesmas’ $780,000: send them to the brokerage partner or tell him to spread it across banks himself (volume −0.5%); explain coverage by ownership category with the FDIC’s estimator — $250,000 in his name, $250,000 in his wife Rosaura’s name and $280,000 in a joint account, $140,000 for each co-owner, all insured — and put it in writing ($5,000; volume +0.5%; households +2); tell him “everything here is FDIC insured” and open one account in his name (1.5 findings; households −1); or one $780,000 certificate of deposit in his name without discussing the limit, leaving $530,000 uninsured (half a finding; households −1).`,
            r3_note: `Breaking up cash deposits to avoid the $10,000 report is a federal crime for the customer and for anyone who helps (31 U.S.C. § 5324): it caps the score at 35. The FDIC insures up to $250,000 per depositor, per bank, for each ownership category (12 CFR Part 330).`,
            r3_title: `Round 3 · Tuesday 6 July 2027: the cash and the insurance`,
            r4_brief: `On Monday 23 August regional proposed closing the 35th Avenue office, used by about 1,900 households in a moderate-income tract. On Wednesday 15 September internal audit found personal accounts and debit cards opened in 2025–2027 without customers’ signatures by two bankers chasing the goals; the exposure starts at $40,000 and grows with the 2027 incentive plan. The office: close it with the 90-day notice to the FDIC and to customers, a notice posted in the office 30 days before and the ATM kept (saves $180,000; volume −2%; CRA index −8; households −3); keep it with shorter hours, an interactive teller machine and a bilingual banker three days a week ($60,000; CRA index +6; households +2); close it in 30 days with a letter, without the 90-day notice (saves $190,000; about $25,000 of remediation; 1.5 findings; CRA index −12; households −5); or convert it to a full-service branch ($220,000; volume +2%; CRA index +8; households +3). The accounts nobody asked for: fire the two bankers and refund the fees the audit found (0.6 times the exposure plus $10,000; 1 finding); close every unauthorized account, refund all fees with interest, review every banker, correct affected credit reports, report it through the bank’s compliance and complaint process and change the incentive (the full exposure plus $30,000; households +1); close them quietly with no refunds and no report (0.3 times; 2 findings; households −3); or keep them open and reclassify them as customer-requested to protect the branch’s numbers (2.5 times; 3 findings; households −4).`,
            r4_note: `An insured bank must notify its supervisor and its customers at least 90 days before closing a branch (12 U.S.C. § 1831r-1). Keeping unauthorized accounts open and falsifying their origin is fraud: it caps the score at 35.`,
            r4_title: `Round 4 · Monday 4 October 2027: the satellite office and the audit`,
